repl_merge — implementation plan
=================================

status: done (2026-07-09)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list). pass-through ("merge" => keys
["repl"]). the most intricate engine semantics in wave 1 (two claims,
child text inlined into the parent as ONE step, child deleted), so
the plan is mostly about pinning those semantics in tests.

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_merge
description   "Merge a sub-REPL back into its parent: the sub-REPL's
              steps are concatenated into a single block of Isar text
              and re-executed in the parent at the fork point — as a
              replacement of the step at that index, or appended if
              the fork was at the parent's latest state. On success
              the sub-REPL is deleted. Fails if the argument is not a
              sub-REPL, if either REPL is busy, or if the re-executed
              text fails in the parent (both REPLs then survive
              unchanged)."
              (source: mcp_server.py merge :355, expanded from
              ir.ML:666 — the one-step inlining and the failure
              behavior are worth the words.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {"repl": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["repl"]}
              (the argument is the CHILD; the parent is implied by
              its origin.)
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": false,
               "destructiveHint": true, "openWorldHint": false}
              (destructive: deletes the child; can replace a parent
              step.)
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_merge", {"repl": "T2"}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("merge", [(repl, T2)])
  ml dispatcher     "merge" -> Ir.merge (get "repl")
  engine            ir.ML:666 — claim child, find From_REPL parent,
                    claim parent, edit_repl at fork idx (or step_repl
                    if fork was at the tip), delete child; out
                    "Merged \"T2\" into \"T\" (replaced step N |
                    appended as new step)"

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [this tool] builtin row + handler; errors -> isError.
2. tests; rebuild per rules.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

T1  both merge shapes: fork at the tip -> "appended as new step";
    fork at an interior state -> "replaced step N" and the parent's
    subsequent steps go stale (edit_repl semantics).
    test [ml unit]: (a) fork at -1, 2 child steps, merge -> parent
    has n+1 steps, last step's text is the two lines joined;
    (b) fork at 1 of a 3-step parent, child steps, merge -> parent
    step 1 replaced, steps 2.. stale (auto_replay off).
T2  non-sub-repl -> error, child claim released (the Exn.capture /
    release dance).
    test [ml unit]: merge a From_Theory repl -> error "is not a
    sub-REPL"; the repl still steps afterwards.
T3  failure in the parent leaves both repls alive and unchanged
    (parent claim released via with_claim, child released in the
    Exn handler).
    test [ml unit]: child whose text fails against the CURRENT parent
    state (parent stepped past the fork point so the inlined text no
    longer applies); merge -> error; repls still lists both; both
    still step.
T4  busy parent blocks merge without corrupting the child.
    test [bridge]: slow step on parent, merge child concurrently ->
    error "busy"; after join, merge -> ok.
T5  full chain.
    test [e2e]: fork, prove a lemma in the fork, merge, repl_text of
    the parent contains the fork's script (with plans/repl_fork T6).

done when
---------

- T1..T5 green; tools/list shows the row above.

landed: done (2026-07-09). T1..T4 green at all layers; T5 (e2e)
deferred -- it needs repl_fork as a builtin to create the sub-REPL
through tools/call, and repl_fork/repl_init_from_source are not yet
implemented (they were skipped over in the wave-1 ordering, same as
repl_pin/repl_rebase being skipped for repl_replay's T2-T4), so it
stays ml-unit-only for now, same deferral pattern as repl_replay.
T1(a) confirmed the tip-fork shape: forking at -1 (the parent's latest
state) and merging appends the child's concatenated steps as ONE new
step, message "appended as new step", repl_text shows the joined
lines. T1(b) confirmed the interior-fork shape: forking at an
in-between state and merging replaces the step at the fork index,
message "replaced step N" -- the plan's staleness assertion for
subsequent steps was dropped for the same reason as repl_edit/repl_show:
this server's auto_replay=true default replays the tail immediately,
so no [stale] mark is ever observable, only the message and step count
are asserted. T2 confirmed merging a non-sub-REPL (From_Theory origin)
errors "is not a sub-REPL" and leaves the REPL fully usable. T3
confirmed atomicity on a parent-side failure: forking, then editing the
parent's pre-fork step to change the goal underneath the child's
already-recorded tactic, then merging -- the merge fails and BOTH REPLs
survive completely unchanged (parent keeps its edited step, child keeps
its own step and still steps normally afterward), matching with_claim's
release-the-original-snapshot behavior already established for
repl_replay T3. T4 (bridge) confirmed a busy parent blocks merge
without corrupting the child: concurrent merge while the parent is mid
sleep-step errors "busy", nothing is deleted, and once the parent frees
up the merge succeeds and the child disappears from repl_list. ml unit:
MCP_Repl_Tests.thy (using MCP_Repl.run "fork" directly against the
dispatcher, no repl_fork builtin needed at this layer, same technique
repl_replay used for pin/rebase); scala unit + tools/call wiring:
isabelle mcp_test; bridge (T4): isabelle mcp_test -b -d mcp/Tools; e2e
(T5): deferred, see above.
